  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nThrough Thanksgiving Day: Latest satellite imagery/RAP analysis \nshowed the circulation around a closed low spinning over the central \nPlains and a shortwave trough rounding its base over the western \nhalf of our CWA. The shortwave will kick into Alabama later this \nafternoon while the closed low drops southeast and becomes centered \nover northern Mississippi by sunrise Thursday morning. Drier air \nwrapping in the wake of the shortwave will bring and end to the \nrain. Local radars showed rain across the northeast half of the CWA \nat noon with the heaviest in a band along Interstate 59. The rain \nwill continue to taper off from the southwest through tonight and \nend before sunrise in the northeast. Latest surface analysis had an \n1010mb low over the northeast Gulf that will deepen as it moves \nnortheast through Thursday.\n\nThe pressure gradient between the surface low to our east and a \nstout >1038mb high to our northwest will result in nearly a 7mb \ndifference across our CWA. This will lead to a gusty northwest \nwind chills in the 30s and 40s most of the day. The closed low \nwill swing from northern Mississippi and across the Tennessee \nvalley to the southern Appalachians by Thursday evening. This will\nhelp draw the surface high to our northeast closer to our CWA. \nThe result will be a cooler than normal day again with highs only \nin the 40s across the northeast and lower 50s elsewhere. In the \nwake of the short wave trough and backside of the surface low, \ntemperatures tonight will be cooler than normal as well with \nmorning lows in the 30s areawide and subfreezing temperatures in \nspots across our north. /22/\n\nA cold advection pattern will continue into Thanksgiving Night as \nsurface high pressure builds over the forecast area. With the \nskies clearing out, we will see greater potential for freezing \ntemperatures, with mid/upper 20s possible, especially over north \ncentral into northeast MS. After a sunny but cool Friday, another\nfreeze is likely for Saturday night. Going into the weekend, the \ntrough will depart the area allowing much milder temperatures to \nreturn, then a fast moving Pacific stream trough will bring our \nnext rain chances by Sunday. It appears the quick approach and \npassage of the system will limit warm advection and resulting \ninstability, so we will continue to monitor guidance for any \nsevere storm potential and hold off on messaging. Following the\nSunday system, dry conditions with seasonably mild days and cool \nnights can be expected from Monday through next Wednesday. /EC/",
